  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of liquid–liquid phase separation (LLPS) in a cylindrical sample, showing how one polymer-rich network separates from another component and how salt affects that partitioning. In the left example, two intertwined polymer-like chains, drawn in red and blue with matching round and square markers, start mixed throughout the container. After LLPS, the system splits into two stacked phases: an upper region enriched in the red network and red particles, and a lower region enriched in the blue network and blue particles. This illustrates demixing into compositionally distinct liquid phases.\n\nThe right example adds small yellow and green spheres labeled as salt. Before separation, the salt ions are dispersed through the sample along with the red polymer network. After LLPS, the red polymer concentrates into the upper phase, while most of the salt appears excluded from that condensed region and remains in the lower dilute phase, with only a smaller amount retained in the top layer. Overall, the figure conveys that LLPS can create coexisting compartments with different molecular compositions, and that dissolved salts partition unevenly between the condensed and dilute phases.",
